THE NETWORKS SHOULD TUNE INTO UTAH'S FREAK SHOW
By Robert Kirby, The Salt Lake Tribune, August 13, 1998

No doubt about it, I have got to get my own talk show.  Enough of this
column-writing stuff, I want the real money -- the money you can get
from interviewing losers, idiots and Martians on daytime television.

This morning, I watched Montel Williams interview a guy who willingly
admitted to having sex with his girlfriend's sister.  Never mind that
the girlfriend was sitting next to him crying her eyes out, the guy said
he wasn't going to stop.

"This is just the way I am," Mr. Rat Bag said, accompanied by boos from
the audience.  "I'm not going to stop.  I love her [both dim bulb girls]
but I love myself more."

Before we go any further with this, I would like to take a moment to
address those of you who oppose televised executions.  You people need
to watch these talk shows.  Seeing somebody get electrocuted on
television is way more wholesome than watching freaks who ought to be
electrocuted.

Where was  I? Oh, yeah, my own talk show.  Watching Montel, I concluded
that I could do what he does without much trouble (or even thought, for
that matter).  After all, these people are their own show.

Seriously.  What kind of host do you need when the guest is a 300-pound
truck driver wearing a cocktail dress?  Just point the camera at him and
let the audience take over.  Ditto guys who date their girlfriends'
fathers, plus-size women who hang-glide in a bus and people who marry
bison.

My show, which I've decided to call the "Kirb & Gutter Show," would
feature Utah people/loons and their issues/dementia.  Hey, the national
scene is already covered.  Besides, I think people are much more
interested in who is nuts in their own neighborhood.

It's one thing to watch Ricki Lake and conclude that people in another
state far, far away are psychotic, so don't go there, and another to
realize that there are lots of people right here in Utah who need to
spend long periods of time wrapped in a cargo net.

After giving it some serious thought, or at least the same amount of
thought that goes into any other talk show, I've come up with a sample
list of topics for the "Kirb & Gutter Show."  You people over at
Channels 2, 4, 5 and 13 better be paying attention.  If you work at
KBYU, stop reading now.

-- "I change my baby's diapers in public and don't care who it
nauseates."

-- Think it doesn't matter that you kept that new John Grisham novel
longer than you should have?  Don't do it until you see "Librarians who
spank."

-- "Confessions of a testimony hog."  Churchgoers who think they are
blessed per minute at the mike.

-- "I hate Utah but I'm staying and you can't make me move."  See what's
up with the Trapped in Zion on Purpose crowd.

-- "Zion's Call Girls."  The agony of women who can only love guys
waiting for mission calls.

-- "Telestial spirits and proud of it."  BYU students who root for the
University of Utah.

-- "I may be slow, but I'm ahead of you."  Remedial lives of plodders
stuck in the freeway fast lane.

-- "Coping with Genealogy Grief."  What happens to those who trace their
ancestry back to Fred Flintstone?

There are a million stories in the naked valley and only the "Kirb &
Gutter Show" would cover them all.  No more messing around with crazies
you will never actually meet in person.  Tune in and watch people you
know and may even be related to.

If this doesn't fly, I'm going to work on a Utah soap opera.  I have
this killer idea:  "All My Children And Wives."

